{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795714490,"nonce":"DJuK8FyJR7X6xpVz8y5y8jQKH64=","title":"A vector Lagrange field of degree 2 or more next to an N1E field cannot take an essential condition","message":"**What goes wrong.** A vector Lagrange field (`value_shape=(3,)`) of degree 2 or higher declared next to an N1E (or other non-nodal) field assembles, but an essential (Dirichlet) condition on it is refused. At degree 1 the same condition works (it pins the selected components). On main the combination does not assemble at all (#314).\n\n**Reproduce.** Measured on the unmerged branch named in the label. An N1E field E (curl-curl plus mass) and a vector field U of order 2 (mass plus gradient energy), coupled through `E . U`, with `U = (x, 0, y)` on the whole boundary, on a unit box:\n\n```python\nU, V = d.fem_symbols(value_shape=(3,), names=(\"U\", \"V\"), order=2)\n...\nterms.append(U.bind(**B) - vec(*g))   # B: the boundary coordinates\njno.fem(terms)\n```\n\nThe build raises `NotImplementedError: jno.fem (non-nodal): an essential condition on the vector Lagrange field of degree \u003e= 2 (region ...) is not wired next to a non-nodal field -- only degree 1 is. Use degree 1, or declare one scalar unknown per component.` The branch pins this by name in `test_essential_condition_on_vector_lagrange_degree_two_is_refused_by_name`, and `test_vector_lagrange_beside_n1e_matches_split_scalar_fields` shows that at degree 1 the vector field with the condition equals the three-scalar-field spelling DOF for DOF. I ran those two (both passed); I did not run the degree-2 comparison without the condition.\n\n**Expected.** The same condition as at degree 1 and as on the all-Lagrange (nodal) path: every DOF of the region, per selected component, pinned to the value.\n\n**Where.** jno/utils/solver/fem_nonnodal.py, `_lagrange_dirichlet_pins`: the degree \u003e= 2 branch interpolates a scalar trace with `_general_trace_pins` onto `dofmaps[fidx]` and has no component-wise layout (DOF `node * n_comp + comp`, the layout the field is stored in).\n\n**Workaround.** Declare one scalar unknown per component (the refusal says so), or use degree 1.\n\n**Direction.** Apply `_general_trace_pins` once per constrained component and offset each pin into the component-wise layout. The test above becomes the oracle: the vector field must equal the three-scalar-field solve DOF for DOF, with the condition, at degree 2 and 3.","files":null}]}